# FrameLink Fleet Manager — the values framelink.stack.yml asks for.
#
# ── THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE AND MUST STAY ONE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# It is committed. Nothing in it is a real value, and nothing secret may ever be written into it
# — not the operator password, not an alert token, not a LiveKit secret. Repository rule §1.2 is
# categorical about credentials in committed artifacts, and the Fleet Manager gives that rule
# somewhere to land: §3.2 takes the password from an environment variable only, so the variable
# is the credential and the place it is typed is the deployment tool.
#
# ── WHERE THE REAL VALUES GO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Portainer → your stack → Environment variables. Portainer holds them in its own database and
# re-supplies them on every redeploy, so a redeploy needs nobody to remember anything and no file
# on the Docker host holds a password. Copy the names below into that panel; do not copy this
# file onto the host as a .env.
#
# Docker Compose `secrets:` was considered and is not used. Its file-backed form needs the secret
# written to a file on the host, which is precisely what §1.2 forbids, and its Swarm form needs a
# Swarm. An environment variable read once at start-up, from a store that already exists, is both
# fewer moving parts and a smaller footprint on disk: none.
#
# ── ROTATING THE PASSWORD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Change it in Portainer and redeploy the stack. There is no password file, no hash on disk and
# no first-run wizard to undo — the variable IS the credential (§3.2), so rotation is a restart.
# Frames are unaffected: /agent is exempt from the password and authenticates by keypair.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — the deployment refuses to be useful without these
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The image tag to run. ALWAYS an immutable tag, never `:latest` — this value is the whole of the
# rollback story (docs/14, step 8), and a moving tag makes "run the previous one" unanswerable.
# Produced by deploy/fleet-manager/build-image.sh, which prints it on the last line.
FRAMELINK_IMAGE=framelink/fleet-manager:0.0.0-0000000

# The operator password (§3.2, decision 43). One operator, no accounts, no roles. Minimum 24
# characters, enforced at start-up: below that the instance stays unconfigured and says why,
# because this login is reachable from the internet.
#   Generate one:  openssl rand -base64 33
FRAMELINK_OPERATOR_PASSWORD=

# The public name. Must already resolve to this Docker host and be covered by the certificate
# resolver named below.
FRAMELINK_DOMAIN=framelink.huisman.io

# Traefik's certificate resolver. Verified against the operator's Traefik static config: one
# resolver, `letsencrypt`, over the HTTP challenge. The stack already defaults to this, so the
# line is here to be seen rather than to be needed.
FRAMELINK_CERT_RESOLVER=letsencrypt

# There is no FRAMELINK_GUI_MIDDLEWARES, and its absence is the point.
#
# Authelia in this estate is `authelia@file`, a forwardAuth attached to the HTTPS ENTRYPOINT in
# Traefik's static configuration. Every HTTPS router inherits it with no label at all, and a
# router that names its own middlewares REPLACES that inherited set instead of adding to it. So
# the console is protected by saying nothing, and the /agent route opts out with an explicit
# empty middlewares label in the stack file. Both are already correct there and neither is a
# variable, because getting this backwards does not degrade the deployment — it either leaves the
# console open or makes every frame in the fleet unable to connect.

# ── THIS STACK'S /24 — THE ONE VALUE THAT IS STILL THE OPERATOR'S TO DECIDE ──────────────────
# The registry at config/networks.yaml is alphabetical-sequential: stacks are listed in
# alphabetical order and numbered 172.16.1.0/24 upward in that order, freed slots are never
# reused, and the file's own footer says the next free slot is 172.16.32.0/24 "but only if the
# new stack name sorts after wreckfest2".
#
# `framelink` does not. It sorts between `fossflow` (172.16.7.0/24) and `frigate` (172.16.8.0/24).
# So there is no mechanical next-free-slot answer here, and the two candidates differ enormously
# in cost:
#
#   A. BY THE BOOK — 172.16.8.0/24. Correct per the stated rule, and it renumbers every one of
#      the 24 stacks that sort after framelink, each +1. That includes reverseproxy (23 → 24),
#      which MOVES TRAEFIK'S OWN ADDRESS and therefore every routed pinhole in the estate, the
#      generated /etc/hosts registry, the validator's hard-coded auth-path assertions, and
#      FRAMELINK_TRUSTED_PROXIES below. It also moves homeassistant (10 → 11), which the repo
#      records as having silently broken Home Assistant's Wyoming integrations the last time a
#      renumber touched it.
#   B. APPEND — 172.16.32.0/24. Zero blast radius, and it breaks the alphabetical invariant the
#      registry is built on, leaving the file no longer sorted.
#
# Pick one, then everything else in this block is mechanical. With X as the third octet:
#   FRAMELINK_SUBNET=172.16.X.0/24   FRAMELINK_GATEWAY=172.16.X.254   FRAMELINK_IPV4=172.16.X.100
#   FRAMELINK_MAC=02:42:ac:10:<X as two hex digits>:64
# .100 because the estate reserves .100-.199 for stack-owned containers with the main service
# first, .254 because that is the gateway slot, and the MAC because the rule is `02:42:` followed
# by the four IPv4 bytes as lowercase hex (172=ac, 16=10, 100=64).
#
# Fully computed, so the choice is the only work:
#   A:  FRAMELINK_SUBNET=172.16.8.0/24   GATEWAY=172.16.8.254   IPV4=172.16.8.100   MAC=02:42:ac:10:08:64
#   B:  FRAMELINK_SUBNET=172.16.32.0/24  GATEWAY=172.16.32.254  IPV4=172.16.32.100  MAC=02:42:ac:10:20:64
#
# DO NOT use 172.16.20.0/24. An earlier draft of this file guessed it; it is orcaslicer's live
# allocation, and deploying into it would have two stacks claiming one subnet — the exact failure
# the registry exists to prevent.
FRAMELINK_NETWORK_NAME=framelink
FRAMELINK_SUBNET=
FRAMELINK_GATEWAY=
FRAMELINK_IPV4=
FRAMELINK_MAC=

# There is no FRAMELINK_DFW_RULES either. The firewall intent is a YAML block in the stack file's
# `dfw.rules` label, in the operator's own §4 schema, because that is what their generator parses
# — a one-line string was this project's invention and would not have parsed at all. It also only
# takes effect once a copy of the stack lives at stacks/framelink.yml in that repo, and once the
# matching egress halves are added to reverseproxy-traefik and reverseproxy-homepage; their
# validator fails a pinhole declared on one side only.

# Traefik's address on the bridge it reaches this stack from. The per-IP rate limiter on the open
# registration path (§3.3) believes X-Forwarded-For from this address and from no other; leave it
# empty and every request on earth shares one budget, set it too widely and the budget is
# forgeable. Comma-separated if Traefik has more than one address.
#
# 172.16.23.253 is Traefik's real, single-homed address, and it does not change with the subnet
# decision above unless the operator picks option A (which moves reverseproxy to 172.16.24.253).
# One caveat worth knowing rather than discovering: the operator's HTTPS entrypoint sets no
# forwardedHeaders.trustedIPs, and internal clients hairpin through the gateway by NAT
# reflection, so the client address this limiter sees for LAN traffic is frequently the gateway
# rather than the real client. That is a known open item in their own tracker and it makes the
# limiter coarse for LAN clients; it does not make it wrong for internet clients, which are the
# ones the open registration path is defended against.
FRAMELINK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.16.23.253

# The signalling address frames dial for calls (§3.7). The Docker host's LAN address, not the
# domain: media is published on the host directly and cannot go through Traefik, so the frames
# and the server have to agree on an address that reaches the host.
FRAMELINK_LIVEKIT_PUBLIC_URL=ws://10.20.30.250:7880

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — every one of these has a working default
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where §3.5's alerts are POSTed as JSON. A Home Assistant webhook, which is decision 22's
# "already wired here, no new credentials" — an unguessable URL an automation triggers on, with
# no token to mint or rotate. Unset means alerts go to the container log and no further.
# FRAMELINK_ALERT_WEBHOOK=http://10.20.30.250:8123/api/webhook/framelink-alerts

# A bearer token for a receiver that wants one. SECRET — if you use it, it goes in Portainer
# beside the password and never here.
# FRAMELINK_ALERT_TOKEN=

# Alerting thresholds. The defaults are 30 minutes out of contact, 30 days of remaining call
# credential, a 5-minute evaluation interval and a 15-minute start-up grace before the call
# server's absence is reported.
# FRAMELINK_ALERT_OFFLINE_MINUTES=30
# FRAMELINK_ALERT_EXPIRY_DAYS=30
# FRAMELINK_ALERT_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5
# FRAMELINK_ALERT_CALL_GRACE_MINUTES=15

# Which host address the LiveKit ports are published on. The default binds every interface, which
# is what a LAN deployment needs. Narrow it to the LAN address if this host also has an interface
# calls should never arrive on.
# FRAMELINK_LIVEKIT_BIND=0.0.0.0

# Traefik entrypoint. This estate names its two entrypoints `http` (:80) and `https` (:443, and
# `https` is asDefault), NOT the `web`/`websecure` pair Traefik's own documentation uses. The
# stack defaults to `https`; override only if that static config changes.
# FRAMELINK_ENTRYPOINT=https

# The data volume's name. Changing it points this stack at a DIFFERENT fleet database — every
# adopted frame would reappear as a stranger in the adoption queue (§3.3). Change it only when
# that is the intention.
# FRAMELINK_VOLUME=framelink-data

# §3.9's generated images. They are 3 GB each and default to living inside the data volume, whose
# free space the database also depends on. Point this at a second mount if that volume is small;
# the free-space check refuses a build rather than half-writing one either way.
# FRAMELINK_IMAGE_DIR=/var/lib/fl-control/images

# Point the Fleet Manager at an existing LiveKit instead of the bundled one (§3.7's escape
# hatch). Setting the URL is what switches modes; the key and secret then become required, and
# the secret is a SECRET — Portainer, not here.
#
# THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL ON THIS SERVER. A standalone `livekit` stack already runs there and
# already publishes 7880/tcp, 7881/tcp and 50000-50060/udp — the same host ports this stack
# claims — so the two cannot both be deployed; the second to start fails to bind. That stack's
# inline configuration already defines an API key literally named `framelink`, whose secret is in
# the operator's own repo and must be copied into Portainer rather than into this file. Either
# retire that stack, or set the three variables below and delete the three `ports:` entries from
# the stack file.
# FRAMELINK_LIVEKIT_URL=ws://10.20.30.250:7880
# FRAMELINK_LIVEKIT_API_KEY=framelink
# FRAMELINK_LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
